## 7430ac0e Serve the link over TLS on port 8443, with the visitor's address from the PROXY header

The https of a company's link is to end at the company's own machine. This adds the place it ends: a listener that
reads the PROXY v2 header the link's frpc sends first, does TLS itself and hands the decrypted connection to Fastify's
own http server, so every route, hook and WebSocket works on it unchanged. Every request through it counts as over the
link, and its visitor is the header's source only when the connection comes from the tunnel container, so a visitor's
own X-Forwarded-For and a stranger's header are never believed. Nothing sends traffic there yet; port 8080 is as it
was.

Proved by proxy-header.test.ts, link-listener.test.ts and auth.test.ts, with each listener test seen to fail on a
listener broken that way. main.ts has no test of its own, as the plan asks none and it needs Postgres; types cover it.

Ruling: the plan's test with 30 wrong sign-ins across emails is held as an it.todo, and the same test on today's limit of 10 per email and address runs instead - why: 1b T3's address limit is not in this lane's base, as the map allows - cost if wrong: the seam between 1b T3 and T5 stays unproved until that test is written at landing
Ruling: isTunnelAddress and socketAddress live in listener.ts and auth/tunnel.ts calls them - why: tunnel.ts imports linkVisitor from listener.ts, so the other way is an import cycle, and the plan names no third source file - cost if wrong: plan 2 Task 6 moves two small functions when linkVisitor leaves the app
Ruling: a LOCAL header gives source null whatever family and length it names - why: PROXY protocol 2.2 says to ignore them, and the plan's short lengths concern the addresses of a PROXY command - cost if wrong: a LOCAL header with a short family is taken, with no source to believe
Ruling: the later of two use calls wins, though the plan does not say so - why: a stand-in takes longer to make than a given certificate, so an earlier call could replace a real certificate with a stand-in - cost if wrong: one counter
Ruling: the header bytes for tests are in a new test/proxy-v2.ts, which the plan's Files do not name - why: both new test files build them, and a change to helpers.ts would run the whole server package - cost if wrong: one small file

